  "query_preview": "..Latest IR/WV satellite show mostly sunny skies across \nsoutheast Arizona with just some cumulus clouds starting to form on \nthe sky islands south and east of Tucson. The mid/upper level high \nthis afternoon is across New Mexico with light southeasterly \nsteering flow. Given the moisture profile across the region with \nPWAT values ranging from 1.1 to 1.6 inches, there will be enough \ninstability for isolated showers and thunderstorms this afternoon \ninto evening mainly south and east of Tucson, although we can't rule \nout a stray storm or two as far north and west as the Tucson area. \nMore favorable upper level pattern will be across northern Sonora \nsouth of Cochise County where an MCS is expected to form this \nevening with the northern parts of that MCS perhaps impacting \nCochise County later this evening, so that's something we'll need to \nkeep an eye on. Main concerns with storm activity today will be \ngusty winds and locally heavy rain. \n\nAs the mid/upper level high expands westward into AZ Friday and \nSaturday, temperatures will continue to nudge even higher with heat \ncontinuing to be the main weather story through the weekend. \nSaturday continues to look like the hottest day with a forecast high \nin Tucson of 112 degrees. We should note that if it hits 112 degrees \non Saturday, that would also tie the monthly record for August high \ntemperature. See climate section below for additional information \nregarding record temperatures. \n\nAs the aforementioned high settles across Arizona Friday and \nSaturday, these two days will have minimal thunderstorm activity for \nmost of our forecast area, as the combination of subsidence and some \ndrying will keep isolated storms mostly limited to locales along the \nAZ/NM border and along the Int'l border in Cochise County.  \n\nFor Sunday into next week, the high will start to weaken, and the \nflow will become more northeasterly and then easterly which allow \nfor a ramp-up in thunderstorm activity with a more active monsoon \nperiod expected, especially from Monday onward. Expect modest \n'cooling' of temperatures next Tuesday through Thursday to about 3 \nto 6 degrees above normal.",
